00:47:21.380 --> 00:47:40.190
<v Ira Glass>She does big magazine feature stories for big magazines in the New York Times. But the very, very first big feature assignment she was actually sent out on was by a publication in 1982. The Los Angeles Reader sent out a very nervous, very youthful Margy Rochlin to interview Moon Unit Zappa. You may remember her, daughter of Frank Zappa.
<v Ira Glass>她为《纽约时报》等大型杂志撰写大型专题报道。但她被派出的第一个非常非常大的专题任务是在1982年。《洛杉矶读者》派出了一个非常紧张、非常年轻的玛吉·罗克林去采访月球单元·扎帕（Moon Unit Zappa）。你可能记得她，弗兰克·扎帕的女儿。

00:47:40.190 --> 00:47:45.940
<v Ira Glass>She had consented to her second interview ever after the release of her runaway hit song, "Valley Girl."
<v Ira Glass>她在她的热门歌曲《山谷女孩》发布后，同意了接受她有史以来的第二次采访。

00:47:45.940 --> 00:48:00.510
<v Margy Rochlin>In this little bit that she does on this song, she's using a lot of this language, this sort of Valspeak that no one had ever heard before. And it was considered really exotic. And so I was from the valley, so I was sent to go talk to her.
<v Margy Rochlin>在这首歌中，她使用了很多这种语言，这种以前从未有人听过的“山谷语”。它被认为是真正有异国情调的。我来自山谷，所以我被派去和她谈话。

00:48:00.510 --> 00:48:05.650
<v Ira Glass>She was one of your people. Speak to her in your secret, private argot.
<v Ira Glass>她是你们那一类的人。用你们秘密、私下的暗语和她交流。

00:48:05.650 --> 00:48:18.020
<v Margy Rochlin>Exactly. And of course, what is so touching to me is that I totally bought that. You're right. I'm the right person for the job. I'm going to go speak to her in the valley language. And we will bond.
<v Margy Rochlin>完全正确。当然，令我感动的是，我完全相信了。你是对的。我是做这份工作的合适人选。我要用山谷语去和她交流。我们会有共鸣的。

00:48:18.020 --> 00:48:38.350
<v Ira Glass>So you get there, and you're a bit nervous. And the pressure is on. Which is, of course, the setting for a possible triumph or possible fiasco. And what happens next?
<v Ira Glass>所以你到了那里，你有点紧张。压力很大。这当然是可能成功或可能惨败的环境。接下来发生了什么？

00:48:38.350 --> 00:48:49.360
<v Margy Rochlin>Well, what I noticed was it was a tense situation. I just didn't feel like it was going very well. And the mother was sort of hovering.
<v Margy Rochlin>嗯，我注意到情况很紧张。我只是觉得进展不太顺利。母亲有点在那盘旋。

00:48:49.360 --> 00:48:54.100
<v Ira Glass>Right. Well, we have a recording of it, because you had a tape recorder rolling during this.
<v Ira Glass>对。嗯，我们有录音，因为你采访时录音机在转动。

00:48:54.100 --> 00:48:55.020
<v Margy Rochlin>Yes.
<v Margy Rochlin>是的。

00:48:55.020 --> 00:48:57.620
<v Margy Rochlin>What are some other hang outs in the valley, besides the Galleria?
<v Margy Rochlin>除了画廊，山谷里还有哪些常去的地方？

00:48:57.620 --> 00:49:01.800
<v Moon Unit  Zappa>Bowling alleys with big arcades are very popular.
<v Moon Unit  Zappa>带有大型游戏厅的保龄球馆非常受欢迎。

00:49:01.800 --> 00:49:03.100
<v Margy Rochlin>Like what?
<v Margy Rochlin>比如什么？

00:49:03.100 --> 00:49:11.720
<v Margy Rochlin>Oh, I'm trying-- at this point, I'm sort of that rock bottom level that everyone can get at in an interview, where you're just saying, like, what's your favorite color? And she's trying to help me along.
<v Margy Rochlin>哦，我正在尝试——在这一点上，我处于那种面试时每个人都会遇到的最低谷，你只是在问，比如，你最喜欢的颜色是什么？她正试图帮我解围。

00:49:11.720 --> 00:49:13.360
<v Margy Rochlin>Kirkwood's is gone.
<v Margy Rochlin>柯克伍德的店关了。

00:49:13.360 --> 00:49:15.380
<v Moon Unit Zappa>It's now the sports center.
<v Moon Unit Zappa>它现在是体育中心。

00:49:15.380 --> 00:49:18.700
<v Margy Rochlin>Oh, but it's the same thing?
<v Margy Rochlin>哦，但是一回事吗？

00:49:18.700 --> 00:49:20.800
<v Moon Unit Zappa>Yeah, it's still very popular.
<v Moon Unit Zappa>是的，它仍然非常受欢迎。

00:49:20.800 --> 00:49:41.190
<v Margy Rochlin>So we're seated in the den. And the mother made me coffee. But I was too nervous to drink it. But I sort of kept staring at it. And she kept staring at it. And I felt like it was pretty important that at some point, I better drain that coffee cup.
<v Margy Rochlin>所以我们坐在书房里。母亲给我泡了咖啡。但我太紧张了不敢喝。但我只是盯着它。她也一直盯着它。我觉得在某个时刻把那杯咖啡喝光很重要。

00:49:41.190 --> 00:50:08.940
<v Margy Rochlin>And so what happened was Moon told me a joke. And I didn't see the joke coming. And right before she told me the joke, I had taken a big swig of the coffee, which was now cold. And when she told me the joke, I burst out laughing. And I started to choke. And so I pressed my lips together, so I didn't spit it out. I didn't want to do a spit-take. And the coffee came shooting out my nose.
<v Margy Rochlin>于是发生了什么，月球给我讲了个笑话。我没料到那个笑话。就在她讲完笑话之前，我喝了一大口咖啡，那咖啡现在已经凉了。当她讲完笑话时，我爆笑出来。我开始呛住。所以我紧闭嘴唇，这样我就没吐出来。我不想来一次“喷饭式”。咖啡直接从我鼻子里喷了出来。

00:50:08.940 --> 00:50:10.640
<v Ira Glass>Shooting out your nose?
<v Ira Glass>从你鼻子里喷出来？

00:50:10.640 --> 00:50:12.380
<v Margy Rochlin>Shooting right out my nose.
<v Margy Rochlin>直接从我鼻子里喷了出来。

00:50:12.380 --> 00:50:14.540
<v Moon Unit Zappa>Are you OK?
<v Moon Unit Zappa>你没事吧？

00:50:14.540 --> 00:50:14.700
<v Moon Unit Zappa>[COUGHING]
<v Moon Unit Zappa>[咳嗽声]

00:50:14.700 --> 00:50:18.630
<v Moon's Mother>Are you OK? Get your hands up.
<v Moon's Mother>你没事吧？把手举起来。

00:50:18.630 --> 00:50:40.400
<v Margy  Rochlin>I was really embarrassed, but simultaneously, I couldn't breath. At the same time, I was choking. And I jumped up. And I sort of started running around the room, knocking things over. And I think that they didn't know what was going on. But the mother began chasing me.
<v Margy  Rochlin>我真的很尴尬，但与此同时，我无法呼吸。同时，我正在呛住。我跳了起来。我开始在房间里跑来跑去，打翻了一些东西。我想他们不知道发生了什么。但母亲开始追我。

00:50:40.400 --> 00:50:43.280
<v Ira Glass>She began chasing you?
<v Ira Glass>她开始追你？

00:50:43.280 --> 00:50:54.210
<v Margy Rochlin>She began chasing me, because I was running from corner to corner, trying to catch my breath. And she began chasing me. And at a certain point, she got behind me, and she gave me the Heimlich maneuver.
<v Margy Rochlin>她开始追我，因为我从一个角落跑到另一个角落，试图喘口气。她开始追我。在某个时刻，她走到我身后，对我做了海姆立克急救法。

00:50:54.210 --> 00:50:57.890
<v Moon's Mother>Put your arms up. No, really. Seriously? OK?
<v Moon's Mother>把手臂举起来。不，真的。说真的？好的？

00:50:57.890 --> 00:51:01.140
<v Moon Unit Zappa>Let's do the Heimlich maneuver.
<v Moon Unit Zappa>让我们做海姆立克急救法。

00:51:01.140 --> 00:51:09.790
<v Ira  Glass>Well, I've been in the news business-- I've been a reporter for 20 years. And nobody's ever given me the Heimlich maneuver while I've been on a story.
<v Ira  Glass>嗯，我从事新闻业——我当记者20年了。在我做报道时还从来没有人对我用过海姆立克急救法。

00:51:09.790 --> 00:51:28.730
<v Margy Rochlin>Well, I always say that it's a benchmark. It's a very low benchmark. And I can do any interview. I can get thrown off a set. I can be cursed out by the subject, but I can leave and get in the car, and I can drive home and think, I didn't blow coffee out my nose.
<v Margy Rochlin>嗯，我总是说这是一种基准。这是一个非常低的基准。我可以做任何采访。我可以被赶出片场。我可以被受访者咒骂，但我可以离开并上车，我可以开车回家想，我没把咖啡从鼻子里喷出来。

00:51:28.730 --> 00:51:33.910
<v Ira Glass>Now, what happened after that?
<v Ira Glass>那之后发生了什么？

00:51:33.910 --> 00:51:48.430
<v Margy Rochlin>It was sort of like we'd all been in an earthquake together. And all of nervousness left the room. And suddenly we were three gals, just chatting. And I remember that I sort of hugged them both when I left.
<v Margy Rochlin>就像我们都在地震中经历过一样。所有的紧张都消失了。突然间我们变成了三个姐妹，只是在聊天。我记得离开时我拥抱了她们俩。

00:51:48.430 --> 00:51:49.120
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:51:49.120 --> 00:51:50.830
<v Margy Rochlin>They were now my friends.
<v Margy Rochlin>她们现在成了我的朋友。

00:51:50.830 --> 00:52:12.180
<v Ira Glass>It's interesting, because one of our criteria for a fiasco is that all social order, the normal social structure breaks down. And literally, that's what happens here. The normal interview stops. And the social structure of the moment completely changes. The mom gives you the Heimlich maneuver. And then, suddenly, it stops feeling like an interview.
<v Ira Glass>这很有趣，因为关于惨败的标准之一是，所有的社会秩序，正常的社会结构都会瓦解。字面意义上，这就是这里发生的事情。正常的采访停止了。那一刻的社会结构彻底改变了。妈妈对你做了海姆立克急救法。然后，突然间，它不再感觉像是一次采访了。

00:52:12.180 --> 00:52:33.480
<v Margy Rochlin>Yeah. And I have to say that it was a very embarrassing experience, and it completely made me feel close to them. It was so interesting. When Moon's father died a while ago, I bumped into her somewhere, and we both burst into tears. I really felt like a little sister of mine had had a loss. The starting point was--
<v Margy Rochlin>是的。我不得不说这是一种非常尴尬的经历，它让我完全感到了与她们的亲近。这太有趣了。月球的父亲前阵子去世时，我在某处碰到了她，我们都哭了。我真的觉得像是我自己的小妹妹经历了一场失去。起点是——

00:52:33.480 --> 00:52:35.100
<v Ira Glass>That moment.
<v Ira Glass>那一刻。

00:52:35.100 --> 00:52:36.390
<v Margy Rochlin>--that moment.
<v Margy Rochlin>——那一刻。

00:52:36.390 --> 00:52:52.270
<v Ira Glass>To me, the thing about it that's useful is that it shows the useful purpose of a fiasco. That is, when social order breaks down, that could be a force not just for chaos and for entropy and for evil, but in fact, that could be a force for good.
<v Ira Glass>对我来说，它有用的地方在于它展示了惨败的有用目的。也就是说，当社会秩序瓦解时，那可能不仅仅是混乱、熵和邪恶的力量，事实上，那可能是一种向善的力量。

00:52:52.270 --> 00:52:53.690
<v Margy Rochlin>Right.
<v Margy Rochlin>对。

00:52:53.690 --> 00:52:55.540
<v Ira Glass>It can bring people together.
<v Ira Glass>它可以把人们聚在一起。

00:52:55.540 --> 00:53:18.300
<v Margy  Rochlin>Right. It was actually this huge success to me. I'd never been sent out under these kind of circumstances before. And I remember we beat the local paper. The Herald Examiner followed us a week later. And so we had the first story. And it was sort of considered the definitive one, because we had this glossary of terms that I had made or put together.
<v Margy  Rochlin>对。对我来说，这实际上是一次巨大的成功。我以前从没在这样的环境下被派出去过。我记得我们赢了当地的报纸。《先驱审查报》一周后跟踪了我们。所以我们有了第一篇报道。它被认为是决定性的一篇，因为我们有我制作或整理的一份术语表。

00:53:18.300 --> 00:53:20.950
<v Ira Glass>Of Valleyspeak terms.
<v Ira Glass>关于山谷语的术语。

00:53:20.950 --> 00:53:24.540
<v Margy Rochlin>Of Valleyspeak terms. And then it was syndicated.
<v Margy Rochlin>关于山谷语的术语。然后它被同步发刊了。

00:53:24.540 --> 00:53:28.740
<v Ira Glass>And most of the quotable stuff that you ended up using in your story happened after the incident?
<v Ira Glass>而且你故事中最终用到的大多数可引用的内容都是发生在那个事件之后？

00:53:28.740 --> 00:53:29.390
<v Margy Rochlin>Yeah.
<v Margy Rochlin>是的。

00:53:29.390 --> 00:53:32.190
<v Ira Glass>It happened after squirting the coffee through your nose.
<v Ira Glass>它发生在咖啡从你鼻子里喷出之后。

00:53:32.190 --> 00:53:56.730
<v Margy Rochlin>Right, exactly. Exactly. It's a technique I don't suggest anyone trying. For years afterwards, Moon would send me postcards. And on the postcard somewhere would be a picture of a nose, and there would be liquid coming out of it. Sort of like my logo.
<v Margy Rochlin>对，完全正确。完全正确。这是一种我不建议任何人尝试的技术。在那之后的几年里，月球会给我寄明信片。在明信片上的某个地方会有一个鼻子的图片，并且会有液体从里面流出来。有点像我的标志。

00:53:56.730 --> 00:54:00.350
<v Ira Glass>Margy Rochlin in Los Angeles.
<v Ira Glass>在洛杉矶的玛吉·罗克林。

00:54:00.350 --> 00:54:13.560
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "DAMAGE CONTROL" BY THE IDLE HANDS] [MUSIC - "DAMAGE CONTROL" BY THE IDLE HANDS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - “损坏控制”乐队演唱的《损坏控制》] [音乐 - “损坏控制”乐队演唱的《损坏控制》]

00:54:13.560 --> 00:54:50.520
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Nancy Updike and myself, with Paul Tough, Alix Siegel, and Julie Snyder. Contributing editors for today's program, Jack Hitt, Margy Rochlin, and Consigliere Sarah Vowell. Production help today from Dana Chivvis. Seth Lind is our operations director. Emily Condon is our production manager. Elise Bergerson is our administrative assistant. Adrianne Mathiowetz runs our website. Musical help today from John Connors and Damien Graef.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我们的节目今天由南希·厄普戴克和我本人制作，保罗·托夫、阿丽克斯·西格尔和朱莉·斯奈德参与。今天节目的特约编辑有杰克·希特、玛吉·罗克林和参谋萨拉·沃威尔。今天的制作帮助来自达娜·奇维斯。塞思·林德是我们的运营总监。艾米丽·康顿是我们的制作经理。埃莉斯·伯格森是我们的行政助理。阿德里安娜·马蒂奥维茨运营我们的网站。今天的音乐帮助来自约翰·康纳斯和达米安·格拉夫。

00:54:50.520 --> 00:55:02.970
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder, Torey Malatia, who walks into our studio at the end of each and every episode of our show to grimly assess the damage.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由国际公共广播电台发行。一如既往，感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚，他会在我们每一集节目的最后走进我们的演播室，阴郁地评估损失。

00:55:02.970 --> 00:55:08.810
<v Cop>Dead squirrel, ruined pillows, need a new couch. The walls are covered with soot. The fire alarms are going off.
<v Cop>死松鼠，枕头毁了，需要买个新沙发。墙上全是烟灰。火灾报警器响个不停。

00:55:08.810 --> 00:55:14.140
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass, back next week with more stories of this American life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯，下周带回更多这些美国生活的故事。

00:55:14.140 --> 00:55:19.140
<v Announcer>PRI, Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>PRI，国际公共广播电台。
